---
import Card from "astro-theme-university/components/Card.astro";
import CardGrid from "astro-theme-university/components/CardGrid.astro";
import ContentLayout from "astro-theme-university/layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import heroImage from "../assets/images/hero-home.avif";
import { courseMeta } from "../course-config";
import { sessionLabels, siteConfig } from "../site-config";

// STARTER_CONTENT: replace the hero artwork and its alt text below, then
// remove this comment.
---

<ContentLayout
  title={`${courseMeta.code}: ${courseMeta.title}`}
  description={courseMeta.description}
  heroImage={heroImage}
  heroImageAlt="A lecture theatre reduced to flat gold and black shapes, rows of seats sweeping past the frame, in a two-ink risograph print"
  {...siteConfig}
>
  <ul class="course-tags" aria-label="Course tags">
    {courseMeta.tags.map((tag) => <li>{tag}</li>)}
  </ul>

  <p class="lede">
    Every upload bar you have ever watched was lying to you, and most of them
    were lying for good reasons.
  </p>

  <p>
    A progress bar is a promise made under uncertainty. The system does not know
    how long this will take, and it has to say something anyway. This course
    spends a semester on that one sentence.
  </p>

  <p>
    It is not a course about UX. It is a course about the twelve inches of
    screen between a user pressing <strong>Upload</strong> and finding out
    whether it worked.
  </p>

  <h2>What you will do</h2>

  <p>
    The whole semester is one artefact. In week 1 you inherit a file-upload
    interface that sits at 99% for eleven seconds and then fails silently. By
    week 12 it tells the truth &mdash; including when the truth is
    <em>I don&rsquo;t know</em>.
  </p>

  <p>
    Everything in between is you finding out what your interface actually has
    evidence for: bytes, stages, elapsed time, an estimate, or nothing at all.
    You will build a state model and discover it was wrong. You will write three
    time estimators and measure how much each one lies to itself. You will turn
    the animation off and find out whether it was carrying information. You will
    watch three people use the thing and resist the urge to change everything
    they touched.
  </p>

  <p>
    No server is involved. Every transfer, stall, timeout and dropped connection
    is simulated locally, so you can reproduce any failure on demand. That is
    the thing you can never do with a real network, and it is the reason this
    subject can be taught at all.
  </p>

  <h2>How it runs</h2>

  <p>
    Twelve weekly studios, three hours each, and <strong>four</strong> lectures.
    The lectures are set pieces: each one introduces something you could not
    work out at the machine, and the studio two days later applies it. The other
    eight weeks open with a short framing and then get out of the way.
  </p>

  <h2>Who it is for</h2>

  <p>
    Second-year students who can write HTML and CSS and enough JavaScript to
    write a function that takes a callback. You will not need a background in
    psychology, and you are not permitted to cite one you do not have.
  </p>

  <h2>What this course is not</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Not a general interface design course.</strong> One widget, one
      interaction, twelve weeks.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Not a psychology course.</strong> We read what the published work
      on perceived duration claims, and then we fail to replicate it in a room
      of twenty classmates, on purpose.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Not a course about making users feel better.</strong> Sometimes an
      honest interface is the more irritating one. That trade is the subject.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where to go next</h2>

  <CardGrid columns={2}>
    <Card title={sessionLabels.plural} href="/sessions/">
      <p>The twelve weeks, each with the question it answers.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Lectures" href="/lectures/">
      <p>All four of them, and what each one carries.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Assessment" href="/assessments/">
      <p>Three pieces, 20/30/50, none of them a final exam.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="People" href="/people/">
      <p>Who teaches this and how to reach them.</p>
    </Card>
  </CardGrid>
</ContentLayout>

<style>
  .course-tags {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin-block: 0 2rem;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .course-tags li {
    padding: 0.25rem 0.65rem;
    border-radius: 999px;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }

  .lede {
    font-size: 1.25rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
    font-weight: 600;
    text-wrap: balance;
  }
</style>
